  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This preprint shows early scholarly uptake and downstream reuse for a method that enables in situ fluorescence imaging of gas vesicle nanostructures. The record currently includes one citation, along with evidence that the work has been reused in a later article and in a biomedical article mention. The preprint was also subsequently published in a peer-reviewed journal article.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Bioorthogonal labeling enables a practical route for visualizing expressed gas vesicle nanostructures inside cells, addressing a key limitation in gas vesicle engineering: traditional fluorescent protein fusions are not tolerated by the main structural protein. The paper sits at the intersection of bioorthogonal chemistry, fluorescence imaging, and nanostructure biology, and its core contribution is methodological rather than purely descriptive.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">In the current record, the strongest impact signal is still early but meaningful. OpenAlex shows one citation, appearing in 2024 in a review article on gas vesicles in medical imaging and disease treatment. That citing article places the work within a developing biomedical imaging conversation, suggesting the method has already been taken up as part of the broader gas vesicle toolkit.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">There is also evidence of reuse beyond formal citation. The record captures two downstream reuse signals: one article reuse and one biomedical article mention in Europe PMC. Together, these indicate that the paper has begun to circulate in related scholarly contexts, even though the overall citation base remains small.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The publication history reinforces that the work moved from preprint to peer-reviewed article, which can broaden discoverability and long-term use. At the same time, the available attention record is quiet: there are no documented event-data mentions in the digest, so the evidence for impact is currently concentrated in scholarly rather than public-facing channels. Overall, this is an early-stage but credible signal of adoption for a technical imaging method with potential relevance to gas vesicle engineering and biomedical applications.</p>",
